findings.evidence:
Three places, read end to end:

1. src/lib/create-config.ts:325
       secret: process.env.UNLEASH_SECRET || 'super-secret',

2. src/lib/middleware/session-db.ts:31 - that value is the express-session signing key:
       return session({ name: cookieName, ..., secret: [config.server.secret], ... })

3. src/lib/server-impl.ts:319-321 - the intended per-instance fallback:
       if (!config.server.secret) {
           const secret = await stores.settingStore.get<string>('unleash.secret');
           config.server.secret = secret!;
       }

The guard is `!config.server.secret`, and step 1 guarantees the value is never falsy, so this branch is unreachable in every deployment. Meanwhile src/migrations/20200329191251-settings-secret.js does generate a per-instance key and store it:
       const secret = crypto.randomBytes(20).toString('hex');
       INSERT INTO settings(name, content) VALUES('unleash.secret', ...)

So the project generates a 160-bit random signing key per installation, persists it, and then never uses it. `UNLEASH_SECRET` appears nowhere in the README, the compose files or the Dockerfile, so nothing prompts an operator to set it.

The login handler assigns the user onto the session object that arrived with the request:

    const { isAPI, ...user } = await this.userService.loginUser(username, password, { userAgent, ip: extractClientIp(req) });
    req.session.user = user;

`req.session.regenerate` is called nowhere: grep for `regenerate` over the whole of src/ returns zero hits. The session middleware (src/lib/middleware/session-db.ts) sets `rolling: false`, `resave: false`, `saveUninitialized: false` and does not wrap login. So the identifier that was presented on the login request is the identifier that afterwards carries the authenticated principal. Logout does destroy the session (src/lib/routes/logout.ts:64, and src/lib/routes/auth/reset-password-controller.ts:187), so the gap is specifically at the privilege transition, not at teardown.

The URL is an integration parameter supplied through the admin API and flows unmodified to the HTTP client:

    const { url, bodyTemplate, contentType = 'application/json', authorization, customHeaders } = parameters;
    ...
    const res = await this.fetchRetry(url, requestOpts);

and in src/lib/addons/addon.ts:

    async fetchRetry(url: string, options: any = {}, retries: number = 1): Promise<Response> {
        const res = await ky(url, { retry: retries, ...options });

The declared parameter type is `type: 'url'` (src/lib/addons/webhook-definition.ts), which is a form-rendering hint; src/lib/services/addon-service.ts validates only that required parameters are present (`validateRequiredParameters`) and that the provider is known (`validateKnownProvider`). Grepping src/lib/addons/ and addon-service.ts for `169.254`, `127.0.0.1`, `localhost`, `isPrivate`, `allowlist`, `denylist` or `blocklist` returns zero hits. `ky` follows redirects by default and no `redirect: 'manual'` is passed, so even a host check applied to the submitted URL would be bypassed by a 302. The outcome is fed back to the operator: the handler records `Webhook request was successful with status code: ${res.status}` into the integration event, which is readable through the integration-events endpoints. `customHeaders` is `JSON.parse`d and spread into the outbound request headers, so the request line is attacker-shaped beyond the URL.

    private generateToken(): Promise<string> {
        return bcrypt.hash(crypto.randomBytes(32).toString(), 10);
    }

`Buffer.prototype.toString()` with no argument decodes as UTF-8. Roughly half of uniformly random bytes are >= 0x80 and do not form valid UTF-8 sequences, so they are replaced by U+FFFD before the string reaches bcrypt: distinct byte strings collapse onto the same input. The intent was almost certainly `.toString('hex')`, which is what the same file's sibling code and src/lib/services/api-token-service.ts:400 and src/lib/features/pat/pat-service.ts:105 use (`crypto.randomBytes(28).toString('hex')`).

A correct sanitizer exists (frontend/src/component/user/Login/parseRedirectParam.ts, whole file):

    export const parseRedirectParam = (redirect: string): IRedirectParam => {
        const url = new URL(decodeURIComponent(redirect), `${window.location.protocol}//${window.location.host}`);
        return { pathname: url.pathname, search: url.search };
    };

It discards scheme and host, which is exactly right. Login.tsx uses it on one branch and not on the other, three lines apart:

    const redirect = query.get('redirect') || getSessionStorageItem('login-redirect') || '/';
    if (user) {
        setSessionStorageItem('login-redirect');
        return <Navigate to={parseRedirectParam(redirect)} replace />;   // line 31 - sanitised
    }
    ...
    <Authentication redirect={redirect} invited={invited} />             // line 58 - raw

The raw value then reaches five sinks. Four call react-router's navigate on it directly after a successful authentication:
  frontend/src/component/user/PasswordAuth.tsx:90        navigate(redirect, { replace: true });
  frontend/src/component/user/HostedAuth.tsx:88          navigate(redirect, { replace: true });
  frontend/src/component/user/DemoAuth/DemoAuth.tsx:42   navigate(redirect, { replace: true });
  frontend/src/component/user/SimpleAuth/SimpleAuth.tsx:37 navigate(redirect);
The fifth splices it into a server-supplied SSO path that becomes a real anchor href:
  frontend/src/component/user/common/AuthOptions/AuthOptions.tsx:12-17,68-72
    function addOrOverwriteRedirect(path, redirectValue) { ... params.set('redirect', redirectValue); return `${basePath}?${params.toString()}`; }
    href={redirectPath ? addOrOverwriteRedirect(o.path, redirectPath) : o.path}
The raw value is additionally persisted for ten minutes (Authentication.tsx:38, sessionStorage key 'login-redirect') and read back at the top of Login.tsx, so it survives a page reload.

Two sibling routes in one controller, 55 lines apart. The first is gated:

    this.route({ method: 'get', path: '', handler: this.getUsers, permission: ADMIN, ... })

The second is not:

    this.route({
        method: 'get', path: '/access', handler: this.getBaseUsersAndGroups,
        permission: NONE,
        ... description: 'Get a subset of user and group information eligible even for non-admin users'

and the handler returns every account on the instance, unpaginated and unfiltered:

    async getBaseUsersAndGroups(_req, res) {
        const allUsers = await this.accountService.getAll();
        let users = allUsers.map((u) => ({ id: u.id, name: u.name, username: u.username, email: u.email, accountType: u.accountType } as IUser));
        if (this.flagResolver.isEnabled('anonymiseEventLog')) { users = this.anonymiseUsers(users); }
        const allGroups = await this.groupService.getAll();
        ...

The request object is named `_req`: the handler does not look at the caller at all. `permission: NONE` is not 'no access control' by accident - src/lib/routes/controller.ts:42-56 filters NONE out of the permission array and calls next() when the array is empty, so the route is reachable by any principal that got past authentication. The only narrowing is the `anonymiseEventLog` flag, which is off by default and is an event-log setting, not an access control. Note the third sibling, `GET /search` (line 221, also NONE), at least requires a two-character query; `/access` requires nothing.
